            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has a few air leakage locations that should be reduced in conjunction with upgrades recommended in this report:



** An upgrade to a "direct vent", high efficiency furnace &amp; hot water heater will eliminate the need for the current chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipe - reducing air leakage heat loss through both "pipes"



** The header in the basement is not well sealed



** The basement windows allow air leakage, as does the rear entrance door to the sunroom 



Savings: I have modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage that could potentially save $ 125 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ, and qualify for a rebate through the Canada Greener Homes Program.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 10% reduction I have modeled.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your basement current has 1.5" (R6) of white beadboard styrofoam insulation. The header space above the foundation wall has 2" (R8) of the same product.



I modeled increasing the basement walls with R24 insulation (eg: 2" (R10) of rigid foam insulation against the wall with R14 of Roxul batt insulation in an interior 2x4 stud wall) and increasing the header space insulation by a minimum R20.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the basement walls to a minimum R24 &amp; increasing the header above to a minimum R28, you could potentially save $ 1,025 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 33.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 875 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Basement Insulation - Wall

The Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative offers grants of up to $1950 for adding insulation to basement walls. Insulating basement walls can reduce energy costs and improve comfort. Grants are based on the RSI/R-value of the added insulation and the percentage of wall area insulated. The net increase in wall insulation modeled above (R18) is eligible for up to $1,365. A minimum of 20% of the exterior basement wall area must be insulated.



Basement Insulation - Header

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $312 is available for adding insulation to basement headers. In an unfinished basement, sealing and insulating headers can be a relatively simple task. The grant of $312 is available when RSI 3.52 (R-20) is added to the entire header area along with air sealing. The grant is still available if only 80% can be insulated and air sealed.</foundation>
                <windows>

You have previously had all of the windows on the main floor of the original house upgraded. I have therefore focused on upgrading the other windows:

1. The 3 basement windows (older &amp; not in good shape)

2. The 5 windows in the rear sunroom (older, but still in reasonable shape)



If you proceed to upgrade these windows, I recommend you install ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 3 basement windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 87 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 975 in rebates through the Canada Greener Homes Program.



If you also upgrade the 5 windows in the sunroom, the estimated savings increase to $ 282 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,600 in rebates through the Canada Greener Homes Program.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil hot water tank, that is co-vented with the furnace up a chimney. The combustion air for these 2 appliances is provided via a 6" combustion air pipe bringing in air from outside &amp; dumping it into the mechanical room. I have modeled an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, could potentially save $ 97 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 85% efficient oil furnace, that is co-vented with the hot water tank up a chimney. The combustion air for these 2 appliances is provided via a 6" combustion air pipe bringing in air from outside &amp; dumping it into the mechanical room. I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 73 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (southeast), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,899 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,014 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two older toilets (both 13 litre) that can be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush. Go online and check out some "Youtube" flush capacity videos.



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Installing a Pellet or Wood Stove in the Heated Sunroom



Currently, your furnace is tasked with trying to heat the basement &amp; main floor of the original house, plus the rear sunroom addition (via the 2 x heat ducts from the furnace that are directed to that room). The thermostat that regulates the furnace operation is on the main floor of the house, so not ideal for ensuring a desired temperature in either the basement or the sunroom.



We discussed an opportunity for you to install a wood pellet stove in the rear heated sunroom, with it's own dedicated thermostat.



Savings:

Installing an 85% efficient Wood Pellet Stove used 100% for heating the rear sunroom, is estimated to provide net annual savings (cost of pellets, less the cost of reduced oil use) is estimated as $164/year. This is based on the rear sunroom being heated to 20C by either the oil furnace or the wood pellet stove, and using the Dec. 1, 2021 fuel cost for oil &amp; bagged wood pellets for Fort Smith (see Fuel Cost section further down)



Rebates:

Installing as eligible wood pellet stove can qualify for 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the range hood, 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 6.3 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may be expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace and hot water tank co-vent up a chimney and are susceptible to "back drafting".



During my assessment of your home, I noticed combo smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors on the main floor &amp; in the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer



If you decide to replace bathroom fans, please choose an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



